# SSRF Guard Bypass via IPv6 Transition Addresses in Remote Storage Endpoint Validation

## Summary

The `checkBlockedIP` function in both the Go volume server (`weed/server/volume_grpc_remote.go`) and the Rust volume server (`seaweed-volume/src/remote_storage/endpoint_guard.rs`) validates caller-supplied S3 remote storage endpoint URLs against internal IP ranges. The guard checks loopback, private (RFC 1918), link-local, CGNAT, and cloud metadata addresses, but does not extract or re-validate the embedded IPv4 destination from IPv6 transition addresses (NAT64 `64:ff9b::/96`, 6to4 `2002::/16`, Teredo `2001:0000::/32`, IPv4-compatible `::/96`). A caller who can configure or trigger remote storage operations can bypass the SSRF guard using a transition-encoded internal destination.

## Affected Component

- **Go volume server:** [`weed/server/volume_grpc_remote.go`](https://github.com/seaweedfs/seaweedfs/blob/master/weed/server/volume_grpc_remote.go) -- `checkBlockedIP` function
- **Rust volume server:** [`seaweed-volume/src/remote_storage/endpoint_guard.rs`](https://github.com/seaweedfs/seaweedfs/blob/master/seaweed-volume/src/remote_storage/endpoint_guard.rs) -- `check_blocked_ip` function
- **Attack surface:** `FetchAndWriteNeedle` gRPC endpoint, validated by `validateRemoteEndpoint` (Go) / `validate_remote_endpoint` (Rust)
- **Guard also used by:** `guardedDialer` (Go) -- the DNS-rebinding dial-time re-validation uses the same incomplete `checkBlockedIP`
- **Version:** HEAD (both Go and Rust)

## Root Cause (CWE-918)

### Go (`weed/server/volume_grpc_remote.go`):

```go
func checkBlockedIP(endpoint string, ip net.IP) error {
    // ...
    switch {
    case ip.IsLoopback():
        // blocked
    case ip.IsUnspecified():
        // blocked
    case ip.IsLinkLocalUnicast(), ip.IsLinkLocalMulticast():
        // blocked
    case ip.IsInterfaceLocalMulticast():
        // blocked
    case ip.IsPrivate():
        // blocked
    case cgnatNet.Contains(ip):
        // blocked
    }
    return nil  // <-- NAT64/6to4/Teredo addresses fall through here
}
```

### Rust (`seaweed-volume/src/remote_storage/endpoint_guard.rs`):

```rust
pub fn check_blocked_ip(endpoint: &str, ip: IpAddr) -> Result<(), String> {
    let ip = match ip {
        IpAddr::V6(v6) => match v6.to_ipv4_mapped() {
            Some(v4) => IpAddr::V4(v4),    // only handles ::ffff: mapped
            None => IpAddr::V6(v6),
        },
        other => other,
    };
    // checks: IMDS, loopback, unspecified, link-local, private, CGNAT
    // NAT64/6to4/Teredo pass through unchecked
    Ok(())
}
```

Both implementations handle `::ffff:`-mapped IPv4 (Go via `To4()` normalization, Rust via `to_ipv4_mapped()`), but neither extracts the embedded IPv4 from:

| Transition form | RFC | Embedding | Example (127.0.0.1) |
|---|---|---|---|
| NAT64 `64:ff9b::/96` | RFC 6052 | low 32 bits | `64:ff9b::7f00:1` |
| 6to4 `2002::/16` | RFC 3056 | bits 16-47 | `2002:7f00:0001::` |
| Teredo `2001:0000::/32` | RFC 4380 | low 32 bits XOR 0xFFFFFFFF | `2001:0000:4136:e378:8000:63bf:80ff:fffe` |
| IPv4-compatible `::/96` | RFC 4291 | low 32 bits | `::7f00:1` |

## Exploitation

An attacker who can issue `FetchAndWriteNeedle` gRPC calls to a volume server (or influence the remote storage endpoint URL through the filer API) supplies a transition-encoded internal destination:

```
S3Endpoint = "http://[64:ff9b::a9fe:a9fe]/"
```

1. `validateRemoteEndpoint` parses the URL and extracts host `64:ff9b::a9fe:a9fe`.
2. `checkBlockedIP` checks the IPv6 address: not loopback, not private, not link-local, not CGNAT -- all checks pass.
3. The volume server dials the NAT64 address `64:ff9b::a9fe:a9fe`.
4. On a network with NAT64 infrastructure, this routes to `169.254.169.254` (cloud metadata).
5. The `guardedDialer` re-validates at dial time using the same `checkBlockedIP` -- still passes.

